from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('7');add=A.add
add('2.5-01','Mass is a measure of the matter in an object.','It depends on the particles present and their masses, rather than being the gravitational force on them.',
'An object can be moved to a different gravitational field without losing material. Its mass then stays the same in the ordinary school model, even though its weight changes. Record mass in kilograms or a clearly stated compatible unit such as grams. A balance compares masses or infers mass from a calibrated force measurement, so its operating principle matters when discussing another location. Distinguish an object’s mass from its apparent support or scale force in a changing-motion situation.',
'Define mass.','Mass is a measure of the matter in an object.')
add('2.5-02','Inertia is the resistance of an object’s motion to change, associated with its mass.','For the same resultant force, a greater mass produces a smaller acceleration.',
'A loaded trolley needs a larger resultant force than an otherwise similar empty trolley to obtain the same acceleration. This is not because an additional force called inertia pushes backward. The relationship F = ma already connects the mass with the required force and acceleration. An object continues with its existing velocity when its resultant force is zero. In everyday motion, friction can hide that behaviour by providing a real opposing interaction.',
'Define inertia.','Inertia is the mass-associated resistance to a change in motion.')
add('2.5-03','Gravitational field strength is gravitational force per unit mass.','It can be written g = W/m and has units N/kg.',
'If a 2 kg object has weight 20 N at a location, the gravitational field strength there is 10 N/kg. A different test mass gives the same field value in the same approximately uniform region. The field strength is not the weight of every object; the weight also depends on mass. State the location or supplied model when using a numerical g value. The familiar near-Earth approximation is useful for school calculations but is not a claim that gravitational fields have equal strengths everywhere.',
'Define gravitational field strength.','It is gravitational force, or weight, per unit mass.')
add('2.5-04','Gravitational field strength in N/kg has the same numerical value as free-fall acceleration in m/s² for the local field.','This follows by combining W = mg with resultant force = ma when gravity is the relevant resultant.',
'If air resistance is negligible, a falling object is accelerated by its weight, so ma = mg and its downward acceleration is g. The two forms of unit describe different definitions of the same local gravitational effect. A value near 10 N/kg corresponds to approximately 10 m/s². Do not apply the free-fall acceleration directly to a falling object whose air resistance is substantial; the gravitational field remains present but the resultant force is then smaller than weight.',
'State the free-fall acceleration corresponding to a downward gravitational field strength of 10 N/kg when air resistance is negligible.','It is 10 m/s² downward.')
add('2.5-05','Without appreciable air resistance, an object falls with the local gravitational acceleration.','With resistance, increasing drag can reduce the downward resultant until weight and drag balance at terminal velocity.',
'Just after release from rest, drag may be small, so the downward acceleration is close to g. As speed grows, drag increases and the downward acceleration becomes smaller. At terminal velocity, the resultant is zero because drag balances weight; the object continues downward at a steady speed. Its weight has not vanished. Opening a parachute increases drag and can produce an upward resultant while the person is still moving downward, reducing that speed toward a new terminal value.',
'Explain why a falling object can continue downward at constant terminal velocity while gravity still acts.','Upward drag balances its downward weight, making the resultant zero.~Its acceleration is then zero, so its downward velocity remains constant.')
add('2.5-06','Weight is the gravitational force on an object, given by W = mg.','Use kilograms with g in N/kg to obtain weight in newtons.',
'A 0.75 kg object has weight 7.5 N in a field of 10 N/kg. An object of the same mass would have a different weight where g differs. Convert a mass given in grams before multiplying by an SI field strength. A force measured in newtons must not be reported as a mass in kilograms without using the field relation. Near Earth’s surface, g ≈ 10 N/kg is the stated school approximation unless another value is supplied.',
'Calculate the weight of a 2.4 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.','W = mg = 2.4 × 10.~The weight is 24 N.')
add('2.5-07','A known weight can be used to infer mass by dividing by the local gravitational field strength.','A spring balance responds to force and must be interpreted using its calibration.',
'If a force instrument reports a weight of 3.2 N and the field strength is 10 N/kg, the inferred mass is 0.32 kg. Check the instrument zero and use an appropriate range. An electronic balance often displays grams or kilograms directly after calibration, but this does not make mass and force the same quantity. When comparing measurements between different gravitational environments, distinguish a true comparison of masses from a force-based device calibrated for a particular field.',
'Describe how to infer an object’s mass from a spring-balance weight reading when local g is known.','Divide the measured weight by the local gravitational field strength, using compatible units.')
add('2.6-01','Density is mass per volume, ρ = m/V.','The chosen mass and volume must refer to the same material sample.',
'A sample of mass 54 g and volume 20 cm³ has density 2.7 g/cm³. A larger piece of the same uniform material has a greater mass and volume in the same ratio under the same conditions. Density is not total mass alone: a small dense object can have less mass than a large low-density object. Common units are kg/m³ and g/cm³; one g/cm³ is 1000 kg/m³. In a mass-versus-volume graph, the gradient can give density; swapping the axes gives its reciprocal.',
'Calculate the density of a sample with mass 72 g and volume 30 cm³.','ρ = m/V = 72/30.~The density is 2.4 g/cm³.')
add('2.6-02','Density determination requires separate measurements of a sample’s mass and volume.','For a liquid, subtract the empty-container mass; for a regular solid, calculate volume from measured dimensions.',
'For a liquid, weigh the empty dry vessel, then the vessel with the measured liquid. Their mass difference is the liquid mass. Read the liquid volume using a suitably graduated cylinder. For a cuboid solid, measure length, width and height with an appropriate instrument and multiply them. A cylinder instead requires its radius and height. Check that the volume is the material volume of interest rather than an unrelated container capacity. Divide the sample mass by that volume and state the resulting unit.',
'Describe how to obtain liquid mass when a balance measures the container and its contents together.','Subtract the empty-container mass from the combined container-plus-liquid mass.')
add('2.6-03','An irregular solid’s volume can be measured by displacement of a suitable liquid.','The liquid-volume increase equals the volume immersed when the object is fully submerged without trapped bubbles or significant liquid absorption.',
'Read the initial volume, lower the object carefully and read the new level. A change from 35 cm³ to 43 cm³ corresponds to an immersed volume of 8 cm³. Measure the object’s mass separately and divide by that displaced volume. Choose a liquid that does not dissolve or react with the sample in the intended experiment. If the object floats or absorbs the liquid, the simple fully submerged nonabsorbing method needs modification rather than an uncritical subtraction.',
'Describe how a measuring cylinder can give the volume of an irregular solid that sinks and does not absorb the liquid.','Measure the increase in liquid volume when the object is completely submerged, avoiding trapped bubbles.')
add('2.6-04','An object’s average density relative to a fluid helps predict floating or sinking.','For a simple fully immersed comparison, an object denser than the fluid tends to sink, while a lower-average-density object can float partly immersed.',
'A hollow boat can float even though some of its construction material is denser than water, because its overall volume includes enclosed air. Use the average density of the whole floating object rather than the density of one piece of its material. An object of approximately equal density can remain suspended when other effects are small. The amount immersed at equilibrium adjusts until the fluid’s support balances the object’s weight.',
'State the density comparison that allows a simple object to float partly immersed in water.','Its average density is lower than that of the water.')
add('2.6-05','Liquid water has its maximum density at approximately 4 °C.','Cooling below that region makes it less dense, and ice is also less dense than liquid water.',
'As surface water cools toward 4 °C, its increased density can make it sink and promote mixing. Water cooled below 4 °C can remain nearer the surface, and ice forms there rather than sinking as a dense solid. A surface ice layer can reduce further heat loss from the liquid underneath. This helps explain how aquatic organisms may remain in liquid water below an icy surface. Do not claim the whole lake must be at exactly 4 °C; actual temperatures depend on mixing and conditions.',
'Explain how the density behaviour of water helps aquatic life when a lake surface freezes.','Water cooled below about 4 °C and the resulting ice are less dense, so the ice stays at the surface.~The surface layer can insulate liquid water below, leaving a habitat rather than the lake freezing from its bottom upward.')
A.misconception('MIS-06','Mass and weight are different quantities, so changing gravitational field strength changes weight without necessarily changing mass.','For the same unchanged object, W = mg links the changed field to the changed force.',
'A sealed object of mass 3 kg moved from a field of 10 N/kg to one of 2 N/kg retains 3 kg of mass in this model. Its weight changes from 30 N to 6 N. Reporting the new weight as 6 kg confuses a force with matter quantity. The mass could change if material were added or removed, but that is a separate physical change, not the effect of the field alone.',
'A 3 kg object is moved unchanged from g = 10 N/kg to g = 2 N/kg. Explain what happens to its mass and weight.','Its mass stays 3 kg because no material is added or removed.~Its weight changes from 30 N to 6 N because weight depends on g.',guidance=['Correct distinction: mass remains 3 kg for the unchanged object (1 mark).','Reason and corrected force: W = mg gives 6 N in the new field, rather than a new mass of 6 kg (1 mark).'])
C=A.choice
C('2.5-01','Which unit represents mass?',('kg','Mass is measured in kilograms.'),('N','Newtons measure force.'),('N/kg','This is gravitational field strength.'),('Pa','This measures pressure.'))
C('2.5-02','Which property is associated with resistance to a change of motion?',('Inertia','Mass is associated with inertia.'),('Density alone','Density concerns mass per volume, not directly the response of the whole mass.'),('Weight alone','Inertia does not require a particular gravitational field.'),('Speed alone','Mass, rather than existing speed alone, sets this property.'))
C('2.5-03','What is gravitational field strength?',('Weight per mass','This gives g = W/m.'),('Mass per weight','This is the reciprocal ratio.'),('Weight times mass','The product has different units.'),('The mass of Earth per object','This is not the local definition.'))
C('2.5-05','At downward terminal velocity, what is the resultant on the falling body?',('Zero','Drag and weight balance.'),('Its full weight downward','That omits the balancing drag.'),('Its full weight upward','The weight direction is downward.'),('An increasing downward force','The velocity is steady, so acceleration is zero.'))
C('2.6-01','Which expression gives density?',('Mass / volume','It compares the mass with occupied volume.'),('Volume / mass','This is reciprocal density.'),('Mass × volume','The product has a different meaning.'),('Weight / time','This is neither mass nor volume ratio.'))
C('2.6-03','What should be avoided in a displacement measurement?',('Trapped bubbles on the submerged object','They can increase the apparent displaced volume.'),('Reading an initial volume','That is needed for the difference.'),('Measuring the mass separately','That is needed for density.'),('Stating the volume unit','Units are required, not an error.'))
C('2.6-05','At approximately what temperature is liquid water densest?',('4 °C','This is the maximum-density region.'),('0 °C','Water expands as it cools below about 4 °C.'),('100 °C','This is not the maximum-density temperature.'),('At every temperature equally','Density changes with temperature.'))
C('2.5-06','What is the weight of a 0.60 kg sample where g = 10 N/kg?',('6.0 N','Multiply mass by field strength.'),('0.060 N','This divides by g.'),('600 N','This mishandles the mass scale.'),('6.0 kg','This reports force as a mass.'))
C('2.5-07','A sample weighs 4.5 N in a 10 N/kg field. What is its mass?',('0.45 kg','Divide weight by g.'),('45 kg','This multiplies instead of dividing.'),('4.5 kg','This equates the numerical force and mass.'),('0.45 N','This retains a force unit for a mass result.'))
C('2.6-01','A mass of 36 g occupies 12 cm³. What is its density?',('3 g/cm³','Divide 36 by 12.'),('0.333 cm³/g','This is reciprocal density.'),('432 g/cm³','This multiplies mass and volume.'),('24 g/cm³','This subtracts unlike quantities.'))
C('2.6-02','A vessel is 22 g empty and 70 g with liquid. What is the liquid mass?',('48 g','Subtract the empty-vessel mass.'),('92 g','This adds the two measured masses.'),('70 g','This includes the vessel.'),('22 g','This is the empty-vessel mass alone.'))
C('2.6-03','An immersed solid raises water from 28 to 37 cm³. What is its volume?',('9 cm³','Use the volume increase.'),('65 cm³','This adds the levels.'),('37 cm³','This includes the original water.'),('28 cm³','This is the starting water volume.'))
P=A.part
P('2.5-01','State the SI unit of mass.','kg',1,'One mark for kilogram or kg.')
P('2.6-01','State a suitable unit for density when mass is in grams and volume in cubic centimetres.','g/cm³',1,'One mark for grams per cubic centimetre.')
P('2.6-02','Explain why the mass of an empty vessel must be subtracted when finding liquid density.','The combined mass includes material that is not part of the liquid volume. Subtraction isolates the mass of the sample whose volume is measured.',2,'One mark for recognising the unwanted vessel mass; one for isolating the matched liquid mass.')
P('2.6-01','Calculate the density of 28 g of liquid occupying 35 cm³.','The density is 28/35 = 0.80 g/cm³.',2,'One mark for mass divided by volume; one for 0.80 g/cm³.')
P('2.5-06','Calculate the weight of a 1.8 kg sample where g = 10 N/kg.','W = mg = 18 N.',2,'One mark for multiplying by g; one for 18 N.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Put liquid volume horizontally and liquid mass vertically. Plot the six points after subtracting the container mass; the idealised trend passes through zero.',unit='g/cm³',meaning='With mass on the vertical axis and volume horizontal, the gradient is the liquid’s density. If the axes were swapped, the slope would be reciprocal density.',method=['Check the balance zero and record the mass of an empty dry container.','Measure a chosen liquid volume in a suitably graduated cylinder, reading its meniscus squarely.','Measure the container-plus-liquid mass for that same sample and subtract the empty-container mass.','Repeat for several liquid volumes without changing the liquid composition or spilling the sample.','Take repeats where needed and compare the mass-volume results across the range.'],control='Use the same liquid at a similar temperature and the same dry container; correct tare and avoid bubbles or spills.',table='Record volume / cm³, empty-container mass / g, combined mass / g and liquid mass / g as separate columns.',conclusion='Plot liquid mass against volume. A straight proportional trend supports a uniform density over the measured range, obtained from the mass/volume gradient.')
